.TH \f3symbol-tidy\fP 1 "October 21, 2026" "PJL TOOLS"
.SH NAME
symbol-tidy \(em Symbol tidier
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B symbol-tidy
.BI "[\-Xtidy " tidy-option ]...
.BI [ compiler-option ]...
.IR source-file ...
.br
.B symbol-tidy
.I other-option
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B symbol-tidy
is a command-line tool
that can be used to ``tidy'' the
symbols
used in C
or C++
source files
comprising an entire
program or library
by reporting
unused symbols,
specifically unused
macros,
functions,
variables,
and
types.
.SS General Use
.B symbol-tidy
should be run
only on valid C or C++ files,
i.e., files should compile successfully
with a C or C++ compiler first.
.PP
.B symbol-tidy
should be used on both header (\f(CW.h\fP)
and source (\f(CW.c\fP or \f(CW.cpp)
files
since both should include what they use.
(See the
.B \-\-language
or
.B \-x
options for additional filename extensions.)
.SS Use with Make
While
.B symbol-tidy
can be run on a per-file basis,
it can alternatively be used with
.BR make (1)
in place of either your C
or C++
compiler.
In your
.BR Makefile ,
have:
.cS
TIDY_FLAGS  = -Xtidy --verbose=f -Xtidy --error=never
TIDY_CFLAGS = $(CFLAGS) $(AM_CFLAGS) $(CPPFLAGS) $(AM_CPPFLAGS)

stidy:
        symbol-tidy $(TIDY_FLAGS) $(TIDY_CFLAGS) $(SOURCES)
.cE
then simply
.B make tidy
to analyze all your source files
at once.
(For C++,
substitute
\f(CW.cpp\fP for \f(CW.c\fP,
.B CXXFLAGS
for
.BR CFLAGS ,
and
.B AM_CXXFLAGS
for
.BR AM_CFLAGS .
The
.BR AM_
variables are needed
only when using
.BR automake .)
.PP
The advantage of doing this
is that
.B symbol-tidy
will be given all the options
normally given to your compiler
via your
.BR Makefile ,
specifically
.B \-D
(define macro)
and
.B \-I
(include directory)
options
that affect compilation.
.PP
The
.B \-\-verbose=f
prints each source file's name;
.B \-\-error=never
causes
.B symbol-tidy
always to exit with a zero status
even with unused symbols so
.B make
doesn't think anything went wrong.
(See the
.B \-\-error
and
.B \-\-verbose
options below.)
.SS Predefined Macro
.B symbol-tidy
implicitly defines a macro
.B __symbol_tidy__
that can be tested for
in
.IR source-file :
.cS
#ifdef __symbol_tidy__
// ... code seen only by symbol-tidy ...
#endif
.cE
This can be used in corner cases
where other code in the same file
references a symbol
formed via the preprocessor's \f(CW##\fP (paste) operator,
but defined in a header.
(See
.B Symbols via ##
under
.B BUGS
for details
and an example.)
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Option Kinds
.B symbol-tidy
has three kinds of command-line options:
.sp
.PD 0
.nr step 1 1
.IP \n[step]. 3
.BR symbol-tidy -specific
options.
.IP \n+[step].
C or C++ compiler options.
.IP \n+[step].
Other options.
.PD
.SS Include-Tidy-Specific Options
Every
.BR symbol-tidy -specific
option
.I must
be preceded by an
.B \-Xtidy
option to distinguish it from a C or C++ compiler option.
For example,
in:
.cS
symbol-tidy -Xtidy --error=never \-I../lib *.[hc]
.cE
the
.BR \-\-error
option
is given only to
.B symbol-tidy
and the
.B \-I../lib
option is given only to the C compiler.
.PP
For the options below,
an option argument
.I d
means
.IR directory ,
.I f
means
.IR file ,
.I n
means
.IR "unsigned integer" ,
and
.I s
means
.IR string .
.TP 6
.BI \-\-color \f1=\fPs "\f1 | \fP" "" \-k " s"
Sets when to colorize output to
.I s
(which is case-insensitive).
(See the
.B SYMBOL_TIDY_COLORS
environment variable about colorization.)
The choices for
.I s
are:
.RS
.TP 8
.B always
Always colorize.
.TP
.BR auto " | " isatty " | " tty
Colorize only when standard output
or error
is connected to a TTY
(terminal).
.TP
.B never
Never colorize.
.TP
.BR not_file " | " not_isreg
Colorize only when
.I not
writing to a regular file
(hence writing to a TTY or pipe).
(Specifically,
.BR fstat (3)
is called on standard output
and error:
colorize only if \f(CWS_IFREG\fP is false.)
.IP
This is more useful than
.B isatty
since it accommodates the common case of allowing color output to be piped to
.BR less (1)
and still display in color since
.B less
understands
.I SGR
(``Select Graphic Rendition'')
ASCII terminal escape sequences
that produce color.
.RE
.IP
The default is
.BR not_file .
.TP
.BI \-\-compiler \f1=\fPf "\f1 | \fP" "" \-C " f"
Specifies the path to either the C or C++ compiler to use;
or
.B none
not to use any
in which case
paths to system include directories
must be specified explicitly
via one or more of
.BR \-I ,
.BR \-isystem ,
or related options.
While
.BR clang (1)
or
.BR clang++ (1)
are the preferred compilers,
.BR gcc (1)
or
.BR g++ (1)
are also supported.
.TP
.BI \-\-config \f1=\fPf "\f1 | \fP" "" \-c " f"
Specifies the path of the configuration file to use first.
.TP
.BI \-\-directory \f1=\fPd "\f1 | \fP" "" \-d " d"
Change to directory
.I d
before tidying.
.TP
.BR \-\-debug " | " \-D
Turns on
.B symbol-tidy
debugging output:
.RS 6
.IP \(bu 3
If an error or warning message is printed,
includes the
.RI \f(CW[\fP file \f(CW:\fP line \f(CW]\fP
of the code that generated said message.
.RE
.IP
(This is a debugging aid for developers of
.B symbol-tidy
itself.)
.TP
.BI \-\-error \f1=\fPs "\f1 | \fP" "" \-e " s"
When to exit with a non-zero status.
The choices for
.I s
are:
.RS 6
.TP 12
.PD 0
.B always
Always exit with non-zero.
.TP
.B never
Always exit with zero.
.TP
.B unused
Exit with non-zero only when there are unused symbols.
.RE
.PD
.IP
Using
.B never
always exits with a zero status
even when there are unused symbols so
.BR make (1)
doesn't think anything went wrong.
(See
.BR "Use with Make" .)
The default is
.BR unused .
.TP
.BR \-\-no-config-layers " | " \-L
Suppresses configuration file layering.
Only the first configuration file found is used.
(See
.B Layering
under
.BR "CONFIGURATION FILES" .)
.TP
.BI \-\-verbose \f1=\fPs "\f1 | \fP" "" \-V " s"
Specifies what to be verbose about.
The choices for
.I s
are:
.RS 6
.TP 3
.PD 0
.B a
Command-line arguments given to
.B symbol-tidy
and
.BR libclang .
.TP
.B c
Configuration files read or attempted.
.TP
.B C
Libclang cursors.
.TP
.B d
Directory changing to from the
.B \-\-directory
or
.B \-d
option.
.TP
.B f
Name of
.I source-file
only if it has unused symbols.
.TP
.B F
Name of
.IR source-file .
.TP
.B S
Configuration file symbols.
.TP
.B z
Statistics.
(See
.BR STATISTICS .)
.RE
.PD
.IP
Multiple values may be given,
one immediately after the other.
(The order doesn't matter.)
Alternatively,
\f(CW*\fP may be given to mean ``all''
or
\f(CW-\fP may be given to mean ``none.''
The default is none.
.SS Compiler Options
Options that are neither one of
.B "Other Options"
nor preceded by
.B \-Xtidy
are C or C++ compiler
.RB ( libclang )
options
and are passed to it verbatim.
Important C or C++ compiler options:
.TP 6
+.BI \-\-language \f1=\fPs "\f1 | \fP" "" \-x " s"
Specifies the langauge
.I s
of
.IR source-file ,
either \f(CWc\fP or \f(CWc++\fP,
overriding the language indicated
by its filename extension,
if any.
.IP
The extensions of \f(CW.h\fP and \f(CW.c\fP
indicate a C source file;
the extensions of
\f(CW.h++\fP,
\f(CW.hh\fP,
\f(CW.hp\fP,
\f(CW.hpp\fP,
\f(CW.hxx\fP,
\f(CW.c++\fP,
\f(CW.cc\fP,
\f(CW.cp\fP,
\f(CW.cpp\fP,
and
\f(CW.cxx\fP
indicate a C++ source file.
If
.I source-file
has any other extension
or no extension,
you
.I must
use this option to specify the language.
.IP
Additionally,
unlike with a C or C++ compiler,
\f(CW.h\fP files are given directly as arguments to
.BR symbol-tidy .
If you use \f(CW.h\fP for C++ headers,
you'll need to specify
.BR \-\-language = c++
or
.B \-xc++
explicitly.
.SS Other Options
These options are given directly as-is
since they would never be ambiguous
with C or C++ compiler options.
.TP 6
.BR \-\-help " | " \-h
Prints a help message
for command-line options
and exits.
.TP
.BR \-\-version " | " \-v
Prints the version number
and exits.
If given twice,
additionally prints
the set of configure
feature & package options.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILES
A file named
.B symbol-tidy.toml
in the current working directory
or
.B config.toml
in a subdirectory named
.B symbol-tidy
is used to configure
.BR symbol-tidy .
The file is a ``lite'' version of TOML.
(See
.B CAVEATS
for unsupported TOML.)
.SS Tables
The file is broken down into sections
(``tables'' in TOML parlance)
where each section is started by a line of the form:
.P
.RS 5
.RI [ table-name ]
.RE
.P
that is a left bracket,
a table name,
and a right bracket.
If a table name
contains a character other than
a valid TOML key character of
\f(CW[A-Za-z0-9_-.]\fP
(a letter, digit, underscore, dash, or dot),
then the table name
.I must
be quoted.
.PP
Neither the order of the tables in a file
nor the keys in a table matters.
The type of table is determined
by the first key in it
since no two table types
share keys.
.PP
For the keys below,
a value
.I b
means Boolean
(either \f(CWtrue\fP
or \f(CWfalse\fP),
.I f
means a quoted file's relative path,
.I n
means an unsigned integer,
and
.I s
means a quoted string.
.SS symbol-tidy Table
This table
.I must
be named
.B symbol-tidy
and is used to specify options,
some the same as can be specified
via the command-line,
but without having to specify them
via the command-line
every time.
.TP 6
.BI color " \f1=\fP s"
Same as the
.B \-\-color
command-line option.
.TP
.BI error " \f1=\fP s"
Same as the
.B \-\-error
command-line option.
.TP
.BI ignore-as-argument " \f1=\fP b"
If true,
the file given for the table name
is not checked for unused symbols
when given as
.IR source-file .
.IP
This allows there to be files
you don't control
(third-party or generated)
present and not check them for unused symbols
yet still use \f(CW*.h\fP with a shell
.B for
loop.
(See
.B Use with Make
above.)
For example,
the Autotools generated \f(CWconfig.h\fP
should be ignored:
.RS 5
.cS
[config.h]
ignore-as-argument = true
.cE
.RE
.TP
.BI ignore-symbols " \f1=\fP s" "\f1 | \fP[" s " \f1[,\fP " s "\f1]*\fP ]"
When referenced by the file given for the table name,
the symbol name
(that can be specified as a single string)
or one or more symbol names
(that are specified as a bracket-enclosed,
comma-separated
array of strings)
are to be ignored,
that is,
even if the symbols are referenced from the file
and so are apparently necessary,
the include file declaring them,
if omitted,
will not be reported as a violation.
.SS Source Tables
This type of table specifies a source file,
either a \f(CW.c\fP or \f(CW.cpp\fP
file.
.TP 6
.BI elide-includes " \f1=\fP s" "\f1 | \fP[" s " \f1[,\fP " s "\f1]*\fP ]"
Same as in
.BR "Header Tables" ,
but for a source file.
.TP
.BI ignore-as-argument " \f1=\fP b"
Same as in
.BR "Header Tables" ,
but for a source file.
.TP
.BI ignore-symbols " \f1=\fP s" "\f1 | \fP[" s " \f1[,\fP " s "\f1]*\fP ]"
Same as in
.BR "Header Tables" ,
but for a source file.
.SS Symbol Tables
This type of table specifies a symbol name,
e.g.,
\f(CWNULL\fP
or
\f(CWsize_t\fP,
is declared in multiple include files
so including any one of them
meets the
.I include-what-you-use
requirement
thus averting a violation.
.TP 6
.BI ignore " \f1=\fP b"
If true,
the symbol name is to be ignored,
that is,
even if it's referenced from a file,
the include file declaring it,
if omitted,
will not be reported as a violation.
Note that:
.RS 5
.cS
[\f2symbol\fP]
ignore = true
.cE 0
.RE
.IP
ignores
.I symbol
in all files
whereas:
.RS 5
.cS
[\f2header.h\fP]
ignore-symbols = "\f2symbol\fP"
.cE 0
.RE
.IP
ignores
.I symbol
only in
.IR header.h .
.SS Locations and Search Order
One or more configuration files
can be used to configure
.B symbol-tidy
by reading them
on start-up.
The files are searched for
in the following locations
in order:
.nr step 1 1
.IP \n[step]. 3
The path given as the value for either the
.B \-\-config
or
.B \-c
option.
(If specified, this path
.I must
exist.)
.IP \n+[step].
\f(CW$(dirname\fP
.IR source-file \f(CW)/symbol-tidy.toml\fP.
.IP \n+[step].
.BR $PWD \f(CW/symbol-tidy.toml\fP.
.IP \n+[step].
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME \f(CW/symbol-tidy/config.toml\fP.
If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset or empty,
it defaults to \f(CW~/.config\fP.
.IP \n+[step].
For each
.I path
in a colon separated list of paths in
.BR XDG_CONFIG_DIRS ,
the first of
.IR path \f(CW/symbol-tidy/config.toml\fP
to exist and be readable.
If
.B XDG_CONFIG_DIRS
is unset or empty,
it defaults to \f(CW/etc/xdg\fP.
.PP
If no configuration file is found,
it's an error.
.SS Layering
Every configuration file found is read.
Settings in files earier in order
take precedence over the same settings
in files later in order
with the only exception of the
.B add-c-includes
option.
For example,
if in \f(CW./symbol-tidy.toml\fP,
.B align-colum
is set to 35,
but in \f(CWconfig.toml\fP
in \f(CW/etc/xdg/symbol-tidy\fP,
it's set to 41,
then 35
(the value in the first file found in order)
will be used.
.PP
However,
if either the
.B \-\-no-config-layers
or
.B \-L
option is given,
then
.I only
the first configuration file found
is read.
.SH STATISTICS
Including
.B z
in the argument to the
.B \-\-verbose
or
.B \-v
option
prints statistics
for
.IR source-file .
(These are of interest for developers of
.B symbol-tidy
itself.)
.TP 6
.B ips-size
The total number of ignored files.
.B ips-load-factor
The load factor for the ignoted files hash table.
.TP
.B ss-load-factor
The load factor for the symbol hash table.
.TP
.B ss-size
The total number of relevant symbols.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PD 0
.IP 0
No unused symbols or the
.B \-\-error=never
option was given.
.IP 1
One or more unused symbols.
.IP 2
No unused symbols, but the
.B \-\-error=always
option was given.
.IP 64
Command-line usage error.
.IP 65
.I source-file
has one or more errors.
.IP 66
Open file error.
.IP 69
Either could not invoke the compiler
or get the current working directory.
.IP 70
Internal software error.
(Please report the bug.)
.IP 71
Operating system error.
.IP 74
I/O error.
.IP 78
Configuration file error.
.PD
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR CPATH " | " C_INCLUDE_PATH " | " CPLUS_INCLUDE_PATH
Same as in
.BR clang (1).
.TP
.B HOME
Used only when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is empty or unset
to locate a configuration file in
\f(CW~/.config/symbol-tidy/config.toml\fP.
If
.B HOME
is also empty or unset,
the home directory is obtained from the password database entry
for the effective user.
If that fails,
no default configuration file
under the user's home directory
is read.
.TP
.B SYMBOL_TIDY_COLORS
Specifies the colors and other attributes
used to highlight various parts of the output
in a manner similar to the
.B GCC_COLORS
variable
used by
.BR gcc .
.IP
As with
.BR gcc ,
the value is composed of a colon-separated sequence of capabilities.
Each capability is of the form
.IR name [= SGR ]
where
.I name
is a capability name
and
.IR SGR ,
if present,
is a
``Select Graphic Rendition''
value
that is a semicolon-separated list of integers
in the range 0\-255.
An example SGR value is \f(CW31;1\fP
that specifies a bold red foreground
on the terminal's default background.
.IP
Capability names containing upper-case letters are unique to
.BR symbol-tidy ;
those in all lower-case are upwards compatible with
.BR gcc .
.RS
.TP 17
.BI caret= SGR
SGR for the caret pointing to the error on the line above
(as with
.BR gcc ).
The default is \f(CW32;1\fP
(bold green foreground over current terminal background).
.TP
.BI error= SGR
SGR for the word ``error.''
The default is \f(CW31;1\fP
(bold red foreground over current terminal background).
.TP
.BI locus= SGR
SGR for location information in error and warning messages.
The default is \f(CW1\fP
(bold current foreground over current terminal background).
.TP
.BI warning= SGR
SGR for the word ``warning.''
The default is \f(CW33;1\fP
(bold yellow foreground over current terminal background).
.RE
.IP
The term ``color'' is used loosely.
In addition to colors,
other character attributes
such as bold, underlined, reverse video, etc.,
may be possible depending on the capabilities of the terminal.
.TP
.B SYMBOL_TIDY_TEST
Facilitates testing
when set to a non-empty value
of one
or more
of the following letters
(case-sensitive):
.RS 7
.TP 3
.PD 0
.B h
Don't read files
under the user's home directory.
.TP
.B e
Don't read files under \f(CW/etc/xdg/symbol-tidy\fP.
.RE
.PD
.IP
Multiple letters may be given,
one immediately after the other.
(The order doesn't matter.)
Alternatively,
\f(CW*\fP may be given to mean ``all''
or
\f(CW-\fP may be given to mean ``none.''
.IP
Additionally when set,
most paths printed as part of verbose output are relative
rather than the default of absolute.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_DIRS
If set to a colon separated list of paths,
then for each
.I path
in it,
the first of
.IR path \f(CW/symbol-tidy/config.toml\fP
to exist
and be readable
is used as a configuration file.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
If set to a path
of a directory
and
\f(CW$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/symbol-tidy/config.toml\fP
exists and is readable,
then that used as a configuration file.
.PD
.SH FILES
.PD 0
.TP
\f(CW./symbol-tidy.toml\fP
.TP
\f(CW$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/symbol-tidy/config.toml\fP
.TP
\f(CW~/.config/symbol-tidy/config.toml\fP
.TP
\f(CW/etc/xdg/symbol-tidy/config.toml\fP
.PD
Configuration files.
.SH BUGS
.SS Symbols via ##
Symbols formed via the preprocessor's \f(CW##\fP (paste) operator
aren't ``seen'' by
.BR libclang .
Consequently,
if such a symbol is declared in a header is referenced
and no other symbols from that header
are referenced,
.B symbol-tidy
won't think the header is necessary.
For example:
.cS
#include <readline/readline.h>

#define RL_PROMPT_IGNORE(SBUF, WHEN) \\
  strbuf_putc( (SBUF), RL_PROMPT_ ## WHEN ## _IGNORE )

void prompt_create( strbuf_t *sbuf ) {
  RL_PROMPT_IGNORE( sbuf, START );
  // ...
.cE
The expansion of \f(CWRL_PROMPT_IGNORE\fP
will create \f(CWRL_PROMPT_START_IGNORE\fP
that's declared in \f(CWreadline.h\fP.
If no other symbols from it are referenced,
.B symbol-tidy
will incorrectly think the header is unnecessary.
.PP
As an alternative to not using \f(CW##\fP here,
you can add dummy code
seen only by
.B symbol-tidy
(and
.BR libclang ):
.cS
#ifdef __symbol_tidy__
void explicitly_reference_symbols() {
  (void)RL_PROMPT_START_IGNORE;
}
#endif
.cE
By explicitly referencing such a symbol directly
via dummy code,
.B libclang
will ``see'' it
and
.B symbol-tidy
will correctly think the header is necessary.
Because such code is never compiled,
the code can be anything
(but it still has to be legal
and should not generate warnings).
.SS See Also
.nh
\f(CWhttps://github.com/paul-j-lucas/symbol-tidy/issues\fP
.hy
.SH CAVEATS
.SS Unsupported TOML
The following parts of TOML are
.I not
supported
because they aren't needed
to configure
.BR symbol-tidy :
.sp
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 3
Array of tables.
.IP \(bu
Dates or times.
.IP \(bu
Floating-point numbers.
.IP \(bu
Inline tables.
.IP \(bu
Multi-line strings.
.IP \(bu
Unicode.
.PD
